{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusader states of the Levant. He inherited the County of Tripoli from his father Raymond II a position he successfully defended against numerous enemies throughout his long rule. His reign was marked by constant warfare political maneuvering and efforts to consolidate and strengthen the County.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1120. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Montfrang. He received a military education preparing him for the challenges of governing a frontier territory. Upon his father's death in 1155 Raymond ascended to the County inheriting a realm beset by threats from Muslim forces including the Zengi dynasty of Mosul and the Nur ad-Din dynasty of Syria. His early years focused on securing Tripoli's borders and maintaining alliances with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw him engaged in numerous campaigns against his enemies. He faced constant pressure from Zengi who sought to conquer Tripoli and expand his control over the Levant. Raymond skillfully employed diplomacy and military force to resist Zengi's advances often forging temporary alliances with rival Muslim rulers. He also dealt with internal challenges including rebellions and disputes among his vassals. He invested in strengthening Tripoli's defenses constructing fortifications and maintaining a standing army.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Diplomacy* A key aspect of Raymond III's leadership was his adeptness at diplomacy. He skillfully navigated the complex political landscape of the Crusader states and forged alliances to counter external threats. He maintained relationships with the Kingdom of Jerusalem the Principality of Antioch and the County of Edessa. He also engaged in diplomatic negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to secure truces and maintain peaceful relations where possible. His military campaigns were often characterized by a combination of defensive strategies and opportunistic offensives aimed at disrupting enemy plans and protecting Tripoli's interests. He participated in several Crusades including the Third Crusade although his involvement was relatively limited compared to other Crusader leaders.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1194 after a long and eventful reign. He left behind a County of Tripoli that was relatively stable and prosperous despite the constant threats it faced. He is remembered as a capable military leader a shrewd diplomat and a dedicated defender of the Crusader states. His reign represents a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli a period of relative autonomy and strength amidst the turmoil of the Crusades. He was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. His legacy is one of resilience and strategic leadership in a challenging environment ensuring the survival of Tripoli for several more decades.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -1.4362273216247559}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.03204622492194176}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -1.3112935448589269e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.6225699912174605e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -6.437280717364047e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.1427018642425537}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.05837208777666092}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -7.486063259420916e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.867782187531702e-06}, {"token": "9", "logprob": -2.7179348762729205e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.014101742766797543}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.05775904655456543}, {"token": "▁signif